Q: Apply z-index to SVG path A: <p>If you have a dire need to do it, you can define an explicit compositing order using alternate filter effects (as long as you don't need support in Firefox or IE.)</p>
<p>You can define an explicit compositing order within a filter using the feMerge primitive, once you have imported the content you want using feImage.</p>
<p>You can define the filter applied to the dummy rect (wholeSVG) in CSS initially as url(#firstordering) and then change it to url(#secondordering).</p>
<pre><code>&lt;svg&gt;
&lt;defs&gt;

&lt;circle id=&quot;shape1&quot; cx=&quot;20&quot; cy=&quot;50&quot; r=&quot;40&quot; fill=&quot;yellow&quot; /&gt;
&lt;rect id=&quot;shape2&quot; x=&quot;4&quot; y=&quot;20&quot; width=&quot;200&quot; height=&quot;50&quot; fill=&quot;grey&quot; /&gt;
&lt;circle id=&quot;shape3&quot; cx=&quot;70&quot; cy=&quot;70&quot; r=&quot;50&quot; fill=&quot;blue&quot; /&gt;


&lt;filter id=&quot;firstordering&quot;&gt;
&lt;feImage xlink:href=&quot;#shape1&quot; result=&quot;firstshape&quot;/&gt;
&lt;feImage xlink:href=&quot;#shape2&quot; result=&quot;secondshape&quot;/&gt;
&lt;feImage xlink:href=&quot;#shape3&quot; result=&quot;thirdshape&quot;/&gt;

&lt;feMerge&gt;
  &lt;feMergeNode in=&quot;firstshape&quot;/&gt;
  &lt;feMergeNode in=&quot;secondshape&quot;/&gt;
  &lt;feMergeNode in=&quot;thirdshape&quot;/&gt;
&lt;feMerge&gt;
&lt;/filter&gt;


&lt;filter id=&quot;secondordering&quot;&gt;
&lt;feImage xlink:href=&quot;#shape1&quot; result=&quot;firstshape&quot;/&gt;
&lt;feImage xlink:href=&quot;#shape2&quot; result=&quot;secondshape&quot;/&gt;
&lt;feImage xlink:href=&quot;#shape3&quot; result=&quot;thirdshape&quot;/&gt;

&lt;feMerge&gt;
  &lt;feMergeNode in=&quot;secondshape&quot;/&gt;
  &lt;feMergeNode in=&quot;thirdshape&quot;/&gt;
  &lt;feMergeNode in=&quot;firstshape&quot;/&gt;
&lt;feMerge&gt;
&lt;/filter&gt;

&lt;/defs&gt;

&lt;rect id=&quot;wholeSVG&quot; x=&quot;0%&quot; y=&quot;0&quot;% width=&quot;100%&quot; height=&quot;100%&quot;/&gt;

&lt;/svg&gt;


</code></pre>
